WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:07.000
This is the X-2. It was created to fly at Mach 3,&nbsp;
and to address the difficulties its predecessor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:12.000
the X-1, encountered. It featured swept&nbsp;
wings. Angled backwards to reduce the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:17.560
effects of shock waves forming as the plane&nbsp;
transitioned from subsonic to supersonic speeds.

00:00:17.560 --> 00:00:23.440
It pushed the United States understanding of&nbsp;
rocket engines. Helping to develop turbopumps&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.440 --> 00:00:28.640
to throttle the rocket motor from 11&nbsp;
kilonewtons of thrust to 67. Something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.640 --> 00:00:34.520
the Bell X-1 couldn’t do. Its rocket motors&nbsp;
had one setting, on or off, no throttle.

00:00:34.520 --> 00:00:38.360
The X-2 set both speed and altitude records.

00:00:38.360 --> 00:00:43.560
But not without difficulty, shortly&nbsp;
becoming the first pilot to reach Mach 3,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.560 --> 00:00:48.160
Mel Apt began a banking turn,&nbsp;
while still above mach 3,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.160 --> 00:00:53.640
causing the plane to lose control violently.&nbsp;
He managed to eject the plane's escape capsule,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.640 --> 00:00:59.920
but was likely knocked out in the process and was&nbsp;
not able to deploy personal parachute in time.

